WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Agentic workflows often need to build on
what happened earlier. A support agent may

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.520
need the last few messages before it
replies, or a research agent may need to

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:15.120
remember what it already found before the
next step. But agent blocks don't

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.640
automatically carry that context from one
run to the next. Memory is what gives the

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.920
workflow a place to keep the relevant
history so that the agent can continue

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.200
with the right context instead of starting
from scratch. Let's see how to add memory

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.140
to an agent. Here's a support agent in the
middle of a conversation, and the customer

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.140
has just explained who she is, what plan
she's on, and what's going wrong. By

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.680
default, the agent keeps none of this.
Every run starts completely fresh. So when

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.320
she follows up the next day, the agent has
to ask for everything all over again. The

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.360
fix is one setting on the agent block,
open memory, and choose conversation.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.820
Then give it a conversation ID, a key you
choose, and everything said under that key

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:08.020
is kept. Now the conversation runs exactly
as before, but as each turn lands, it's

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.160
saved under the key.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.880
The next morning, run the same agent twice
side by side, once with no memory, and

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.700
once with the key. Before the model even
sees the new message, everything stored

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.640
under the key is loaded back into the
conversation. Then the same question

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:26.640
arrives in both.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.020
Without the key, the agent starts from
zero, and with it, it answers like no time

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.020
has passed.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.980
Each conversation ID is its own separate
thread, so one agent can hold a different

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:46.880
conversation with every customer at the
same time. From here, the thread just

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.380
keeps going, every run picking up exactly
where the last one stopped.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.420
Memory can also be a sliding window,
keeping the most recent messages, while

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:02.440
the oldest quietly fall away. Here is that
window on the stored transcript itself.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.560
The older turns are still there, but only
the last 10 messages ride into the model,

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:13.980
or if you prefer, the most recent tokens.
So that's agent memory. One setting and a

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.880
key, the agent remembers each turn,
recalls the whole thread, and every

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.000
conversation picks up where it left off.
